.TH PRESHELL 1 "2026-09-24" "preshell 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
preshell \- report what a shell command touches, as JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B preshell
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIcommand\fR]
.br
.B preshell
[\fIoptions\fR] < \fIscript.sh\fR
.br
.B preshell --stream
< \fIcommands.jsonl\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B preshell
reads a shell command and reports what that command touches: which programs run,
which paths are read, written or removed, what leaves the machine, and where it
could not tell. That is the entire output.
.PP
It does not decide whether any of that is acceptable. There is no allowlist, no
policy and no verdict here, because whether something should run depends on
context the tool does not have. It is an analyzer, not an approver.
.PP
It never executes the command it is given, never reads your disk and never opens
a network connection. Handing it untrusted text is the intended use.
.PP
.B preshell
is a standalone program, not a library: call it as a subprocess and read JSON
from its standard output. That keeps the GPL on the other side of the process
boundary, and it means callers in any language can use it without linking
anything in. See the file
.I docs/integration.md
in the source tree for the licence boundary, a batch recipe, and a runnable
parent-process example.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-shell= S
How to read the input: \fBauto\fR (default), \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR or \fBprobe\fR.
.B auto
follows the shebang and falls back to bash.
.B probe
tries the dialect the input declares first, then the others, and names the one it
used in the report. An unknown value is a usage error.
.TP
.B \-\-pretty
Indent the JSON for humans. Single-shot mode only.
.TP
.B \-\-stream
Read many commands: one request per line on standard input, one answer per line
on standard output, in the same order. A request is a JSON string, or an object
.BR {"id":...,"command":"..."}
whose answer comes back as
.BR {"id":...,"report":{...}}
so a parent process with several workers can match answers to requests without
relying on line order. Every input line is answered exactly once, including the
ones refused. Requests are JSON strings rather than bare lines because a command
can contain newlines (here-documents, multi-line loops).
.TP
.B \-\-spec
Print this contract in machine-readable form, as JSON. For a caller that has the
binary and nothing else.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the tool and schema version, as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the same information as this page, more briefly, on standard error.
.SH THE CONTRACT
.TP
.B stdin
The command, verbatim. A shell never gets to re-quote it on the way in.
.TP
.B stdout
Exactly one JSON object, or one per line under
.BR \-\-stream .
Nothing else is ever written there.
.TP
.B stderr
Usage, help and diagnostics. Never mixed into standard output.
.TP
.B exit status
.B 0
when the answers were produced, regardless of what they say.
.B 2
for a usage error, with the reason on standard error and nothing on standard
output. Any other non-zero status means the tool itself failed. There is no exit
code that means dangerous: read the JSON, never the status.
.SH READING AN ANSWER
.TP
.B status
.BR Complete ,
.BR Unsupported ,
or
.BR Invalid :
how much of the command was understood. 
.B Unsupported
means the answer is a partial one.
.B Invalid
means there is evidence the shell would refuse the command too.
.TP
.B impact.effects
The facts: 
.BR Exec ", " Read ", " Write ", " Delete ", " Net ", " Spawn ", " Unknown .
.B Spawn
means control was handed to another program, such as
.BR "uv run" ", " npx
or
.BR "cargo run" ;
what that program touches is not modelled.
.TP
.B impact.uncertain
Read this first.
.B true
means the effect list is not a closed set. An empty list must not be read as
"touches nothing": look for a
.B Spawn
or an
.B Exec
with
.BR "modeled: false" .
.TP
.B impact.write_roots
The directories that would be modified.
.TP
.B impact.cwd
The base for relative paths. Absent when the command itself does not change
directory, in which case the base is the caller's own.
.SH REFUSALS UNDER --stream
A line that is not a request answers
.BR {"error":"...","line":N} ,
with the id echoed when the line carried one. That object is deliberately not a
report: it has no
.B version
and no
.B status
field. It means the line was not a request, never that the command is bad.
.SH WHAT THE CALLER MUST DO
Four things, any of which can otherwise produce a wrong answer:
.IP "1." 4
Serialize writes to standard input. POSIX makes only writes of at most
.B PIPE_BUF
bytes atomic, so concurrent writers can splice a line.
.IP "2." 4
Buffer reads by newline. One read is not one line.
.IP "3." 4
Make ids unguessable, not sequential. A guessable id lets a forged answer
displace a real one if anything else shares the pipe.
.IP "4." 4
Do not let other child processes inherit the pipe file descriptor (CLOEXEC, or
close_fds in Python). Whoever holds the write end can inject requests and can
hold the pipe open so the caller never sees end of input.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 2 on a usage error, other non-zero when the tool itself failed.
See the contract above.
.SH EXAMPLES
Analyse a script and pretty-print the report:
.PP
.nf
.RS
printf 'rm -rf build && tar czf out.tgz src\\n' | preshell --pretty
.RE
.fi
.PP
Read it as a subprocess from any language:
.PP
.nf
.RS
preshell < script.sh | jq .
.RE
.fi
.PP
Analyse many commands in one process:
.PP
.nf
.RS
jq -Rc . commands.txt | preshell --stream > reports.jsonl
.RE
.fi
.PP
Ask the binary what its contract is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
preshell --spec | jq .
.RE
.fi
.SH LIMITS
Part of shell semantics is not statically decidable, such as
.BR "eval $X" ", " "$CMD"
or
.BR "base64 -d | sh" .
For those the answer is unknown, never a guess. A report is not a security
proof; it is a factual list that can be argued with, its own blind spots
included.
